X86_64 PublishMovups
(* The record of `publish_movdqa.kl` published and read with `movups`:
   torn reads are observable.

   `movups` is not among the accesses the SDM guarantees to be atomic, so
   Kraken models it as two 8-byte
   single-copy-atomic accesses in address order, low word first. The record
   is therefore declared as two 8-byte words, `uint64_t rec[2]`: a 16-byte
   location would make the 8-byte pieces mixed-size accesses, which the
   model reports instead of answering. For the same reason the reader uses
   `movups` too: a `movdqa` (16-byte) load of two 8-byte locations would be
   mixed-size. On hardware a `movdqa` reader can see the same tear.

   P0 writes `{1, 1}` then `{2, 2}`; P1 reads the record once. Its low word
   is read before its high word, so the reader can read the low word
   before a store and the high word after it, e.g. low 0 and high 1
   (`xmm2` = 0x10000000000000000), or low 1 and high 2. Even under SC the
   interleaving `R.lo; W1.lo; W1.hi; R.hi` gives a torn record: Sometimes.

   herd7 has no `movups`, so the verdict is argued here rather than checked
   against herd.
# flags: -fuel 300
# expect: Sometimes
# Proved reachable: Kraken.Promising.Reach.PublishMovups.publish_movups_reachable (Kraken/Promising/ReachX64PublishMovups.lean)
*)
{
uint64_t rec[2];
0:xmm0=0x10000000000000001; 0:xmm1=0x20000000000000002;
}
threads { P0: writer done; P1: reader done; }
code {
writer:
  movups %xmm0,(rec)
  movups %xmm1,(rec)
  jmp done
reader:
  movups (rec),%xmm2
  jmp done
done:
}
exists (not (1:xmm2=0 \/ 1:xmm2=0x10000000000000001 \/ 1:xmm2=0x20000000000000002))
